        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="&obo;MICRO_0001524"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="&obo;MICRO_0001482"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:definition>A prokaryotic respiratory metabolic process where light is the energy source, and the electron donor is an inorganic compound.</obo:definition>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Wikipedia:Lithotroph

Photolithotrophs obtain energy from light and therefore use inorganic electron donors only to fuel biosynthetic reactions (e.g., carbon dioxide fixation in lithoautotrophs).  These bacteria are photosynthetic; photolithotrophic bacteria are found in the purple bacteria (e. g., Chromatiaceae), green bacteria (Chlorobiaceae and Chloroflexi) and Cyanobacteria. Purple and green bacteria oxidize sulfide, sulfur, sulfite, iron or hydrogen. Cyanobacteria extract reducing equivalents from water, i.e., they oxidise water to oxygen. The electrons obtained from the electron donors are not used for ATP production (as long as there is light); they are used in biosynthetic reactions. Some photolithotrophs shift over to chemolithotrophic metabolism in the dark.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:createdBy>Carrine Blank</obo:createdBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MICRO_0000500 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="&obo;MICRO_0000500">
