                            </owl:Restriction>
                        </owl:intersectionOf>
                    </owl:Class>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="&obo;MICRO_0001206"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="&obo;GO_0008923"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="&obo;MICRO_0001197"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="&obo;CHEBI_25094"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lysine dihydrolase</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <obo:definition>As assay to determine if a microbial isolate is capable of metabolizing lysine under anaerobic conditions.  When lysine is metabolized, the pH of the medium increases due to the accumulation of ammonia and organic amines (e.g. cadaverine).  This turns color of the pH indicator (e.g. bromcresol purple, cresol red) to red/orange.

A positive test yields a red/orange color; a negative test is yellow.

Lysine decarboxylase catalyzes the following reaction:
L-lysine &lt;=&gt; cadaverine + CO2</obo:definition>
        <obo:database_cross_reference>Wikipedia:Lysine_decarboxylase</obo:database_cross_reference>
        <obo:has_exact_synonym>LDC</obo:has_exact_synonym>
        <obo:IAO_0000232>&quot;Lysine dihydrolase&quot; test results found in a small number of prokaryotic taxonomic descriptions is likely an error - it is more likely to be &quot;lysine decarboxylase&quot; test results.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <obo:createdBy>Carrine Blank</obo:createdBy>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>lysine decarboxylase</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>decarboxylation of the amino acid lysine by lysine decarboxylase (sic)</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
    </owl:Class>
    


